# Web UI (`peaky serve`)

The web interface is a **progressive, on-demand** front end over the same preset + build + splatter stack as the CLI. It is not a parallel product surface.

## Intent

| CLI / build | Web (`peaky serve`) |
|-------------|----------------------|
| Batch orchestration (`peaky build`, `-j`, DAG waves) | One unit of work per HTTP request (e.g. one site's viewshed overlay) |
| Runs ahead of need; staleness skips fresh targets | Runs when the user opens a map layer or hits an API |
| Primary workflow from project cwd | Project picker at `PEAKY_HOME`; map/API under `/p/<slug>/`, `/api/p/<slug>/…` |

Same **core functions**, different **scheduling**: build fans out many sites; serve lazy-loads per request.

## Do

- **Reuse pipeline modules** — `load_preset`, `preset_to_request`, `viewshed_workspace_digest`, `run_viewshed_coverage`, `ensure_splat_raster_png`, build path helpers, freshness checks.
- **Share artifact layout** — e.g. `<preset>/build/viewsheds/<digest>/`; serve reads cache when build already materialized the workspace.
- **Keep HTTP thin** — routing, HTML/MapLibre, JSON in `serve_cli.py`; callable helpers in `serve_*.py` that CLI or build could call later.
- **Add features progressively** — expose the next capability as an endpoint or UI action, wired to existing logic.

## Avoid

- Reimplementing splatter, preset parsing, workspace digests, or staleness rules inside serve handlers.
- Batch-all-sites-on-page-load when only one overlay was requested (splatter viewsheds are cheap individually; still prefer on-demand).
- Web-only tunables that belong in preset YAML — read `config.yaml` like the CLI does.
- A second code path for the same output artifact unless the CLI path is genuinely wrong.

## Example (viewsheds)

Build: configure all site workspaces → `run_viewshed_batch` / per-site nodes in the DAG.

Serve: `GET /api/p/<slug>/viewsheds/<site>` → `ensure_site_viewshed_png` → same workspace dir, `run_viewshed_coverage` when not cached.

